1
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:08,831
In December 2023, shoppers noticed something
strange outside several British stores.

2
00:00:09,438 --> 00:00:12,314
A familiar name had been
reduced to three letters.

3
00:00:12,892 --> 00:00:15,800
WHSmith had become WHS.

4
00:00:16,310 --> 00:00:20,515
The new signs were supposed to help
modernize the shopping experience.

5
00:00:21,017 --> 00:00:25,591
Instead, people began comparing them to
the branding of Britain's National Health

6
00:00:25,631 --> 00:00:26,631
Service.

7
00:00:27,181 --> 00:00:31,983
A retailer with more than two centuries of
history had created a surprisingly public

8
00:00:32,023 --> 00:00:33,953
argument over three letters.

9
00:00:34,486 --> 00:00:37,095
But the real story wasn't
just about a logo.

10
00:00:37,606 --> 00:00:42,018
It was about what happens when a business
changes faster than its identity.

11
00:00:43,818 --> 00:00:50,471
WHSmith's history began in London in 1792,
when Henry Walton Smith and his wife Anna

12
00:00:50,511 --> 00:00:53,021
opened a small retail business.

13
00:00:53,641 --> 00:00:58,157
Over generations, the name became
familiar across British high streets.

14
00:00:58,711 --> 00:01:04,202
Newspapers, books, stationery, magazines,
and everyday essentials made its stores

15
00:01:04,242 --> 00:01:06,032
part of ordinary life.

16
00:01:06,496 --> 00:01:10,747
The company also developed a presence in
travel retail, opening a railway-station

17
00:01:10,787 --> 00:01:13,157
shop in 1848.

18
00:01:13,856 --> 00:01:18,565
That history created something competitors
could not simply purchase: recognition.

19
00:01:19,311 --> 00:01:22,420
Customers knew the name before
they entered the store.

20
00:01:22,829 --> 00:01:26,955
And that familiarity would eventually
become central to the controversy.

21
00:01:28,818 --> 00:01:33,451
By the twenty-first century, Britain's
traditional high streets were under pressure.

22
00:01:33,982 --> 00:01:38,689
Online shopping, changing consumer habits,
and competition from supermarkets and

23
00:01:38,729 --> 00:01:41,779
specialist retailers had
transformed the environment.

24
00:01:42,378 --> 00:01:48,631
WHSmith's stores still occupied recognizable
locations, but recognition alone could

25
00:01:48,671 --> 00:01:50,561
not guarantee growth.

26
00:01:51,088 --> 00:01:55,347
Customers had more alternatives for
books, stationery, snacks, and everyday

27
00:01:55,387 --> 00:01:56,387
purchases.

28
00:01:56,795 --> 00:02:01,534
Meanwhile, WHSmith was developing a
different business in airports, railway

29
00:02:01,884 --> 00:02:03,814
stations, and hospitals.

30
00:02:04,401 --> 00:02:09,245
The company was increasingly operating
in two distinct retail environments.

31
00:02:09,769 --> 00:02:12,953
One depended on traditional
town-center shopping.

32
00:02:13,527 --> 00:02:16,483
The other served people
already traveling somewhere.

33
00:02:18,358 --> 00:02:24,753
Near the end of 2023, WHSmith began testing
new storefront signs at ten locations in

34
00:02:24,793 --> 00:02:25,793
England.

35
00:02:26,213 --> 00:02:31,045
The most noticeable change
was the shortened name: WHS.

36
00:02:31,462 --> 00:02:36,423
The signs also highlighted products available
inside the stores and included local

37
00:02:36,463 --> 00:02:37,463
information.

38
00:02:37,940 --> 00:02:42,639
According to the company's explanation,
some shoppers did not fully understand the

39
00:02:42,679 --> 00:02:44,529
range of merchandise it offered.

40
00:02:44,992 --> 00:02:47,621
The trial was intended to
make that offer clearer.

41
00:02:48,082 --> 00:02:52,767
This was not a nationwide
replacement of the WHSmith brand.

42
00:02:52,807 --> 00:02:54,399
It was a limited experiment.

43
00:02:55,067 --> 00:02:59,391
But a small experiment can still
create a very large public reaction.

44
00:03:01,273 --> 00:03:04,397
Photographs of the new
signs spread online.

45
00:03:05,088 --> 00:03:09,858
Critics noticed that the abbreviated name,
presented in white lettering against

46
00:03:09,898 --> 00:03:13,348
blue, reminded them of the NHS identity.

47
00:03:13,909 --> 00:03:19,247
Others questioned why a recognizable retailer
would remove the most distinctive part

48
00:03:19,287 --> 00:03:20,287
of its name.

49
00:03:20,715 --> 00:03:24,978
The reaction was often mocking, but
beneath the jokes was a serious branding

50
00:03:25,018 --> 00:03:26,018
question.

51
00:03:26,253 --> 00:03:31,139
What does a company gain by shortening a
name that customers already understand?

52
00:03:31,748 --> 00:03:33,044
And what might it lose?

53
00:03:33,705 --> 00:03:37,481
WHSmith had attempted to
communicate its offer more clearly.

54
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,222
Some observers believed the new identity
had made the business less recognizable

55
00:03:42,262 --> 00:03:43,472
instead.

56
00:03:45,284 --> 00:03:48,018
A brand name performs
several jobs at once.

57
00:03:48,555 --> 00:03:53,628
It helps customers recognize a business,
remember previous experiences, and

58
00:03:53,668 --> 00:03:55,338
distinguish it from competitors.

59
00:03:56,029 --> 00:04:00,403
Familiarity can reduce the effort
required to make a shopping decision.

60
00:04:00,764 --> 00:04:02,433
But branding also evolves.

61
00:04:03,053 --> 00:04:08,296
Companies shorten names, simplify symbols,
and update storefronts for practical

62
00:04:08,336 --> 00:04:09,336
reasons.

63
00:04:09,715 --> 00:04:12,723
None of those changes is
automatically a mistake.

64
00:04:13,218 --> 00:04:17,718
The question is whether the new design
improves the customer's understanding.

65
00:04:18,186 --> 00:04:23,242
WHSmith's trial exposed the tension
between simplicity and recognition.

66
00:04:23,667 --> 00:04:25,971
Removing letters made the sign shorter.

67
00:04:26,630 --> 00:04:29,167
It did not necessarily
make the business clearer.

68
00:04:30,915 --> 00:04:33,702
The criticism moved beyond local shoppers.

69
00:04:34,398 --> 00:04:38,291
National newspapers and design
publications covered the experiment.

70
00:04:38,821 --> 00:04:43,402
Some commentators treated it as an example
of branding that had overlooked an

71
00:04:43,442 --> 00:04:46,112
obvious resemblance to
another institution.

72
00:04:46,723 --> 00:04:51,057
Others acknowledged that WHSmith had
a legitimate reason to reconsider its

73
00:04:51,097 --> 00:04:52,767
storefront communication.

74
00:04:53,787 --> 00:04:57,656
The company had been trying to show
customers what they could buy.

75
00:04:58,084 --> 00:05:02,344
But public attention focused
overwhelmingly on how the sign looked.

76
00:05:02,764 --> 00:05:05,145
This created a communications problem.

77
00:05:05,638 --> 00:05:07,887
The intended message
was about merchandise.

78
00:05:08,744 --> 00:05:12,628
The conversation became about three
letters and a visual comparison.

79
00:05:14,671 --> 00:05:16,577
Here is where the evidence matters.

80
00:05:17,090 --> 00:05:22,027
Public criticism does not prove that
a branding experiment damaged sales.

81
00:05:22,683 --> 00:05:26,704
WHSmith had tested the
signs at only ten stores.

82
00:05:27,202 --> 00:05:31,835
Available reporting documents the backlash,
but does not establish a measurable

83
00:05:31,875 --> 00:05:35,185
nationwide revenue loss
caused by the trial.

84
00:05:35,626 --> 00:05:40,711
Without store-level results, customer
research, and comparable control locations, it

85
00:05:40,961 --> 00:05:44,731
would be misleading to assign a
financial cost to those signs.

86
00:05:45,359 --> 00:05:48,371
The experiment became a
public-relations warning.

87
00:05:48,832 --> 00:05:52,188
Whether it was a commercial
failure is a different question.

88
00:05:52,744 --> 00:05:59,005
And WHSmith's larger financial story
was unfolding somewhere else entirely.

89
00:06:00,766 --> 00:06:07,942
In fiscal 2024, WHSmith reported approximately
one point nine two billion pounds in

90
00:06:07,982 --> 00:06:08,982
group revenue.

91
00:06:09,303 --> 00:06:14,006
Its Travel business generated approximately
one point four seven billion.

92
00:06:14,580 --> 00:06:18,243
High Street contributed around four
hundred and fifty-two million.

93
00:06:18,737 --> 00:06:23,297
The numbers revealed how much the
company's center of gravity had shifted.

94
00:06:23,358 --> 00:06:29,108
Airports, railway stations, hospitals, and
international travel locations had become

95
00:06:29,148 --> 00:06:30,918
the dominant revenue engine.

96
00:06:31,306 --> 00:06:35,325
The traditional high-street stores still
mattered, but they were no longer the

97
00:06:35,365 --> 00:06:37,415
company's main growth story.

98
00:06:37,810 --> 00:06:39,617
That distinction is crucial.

99
00:06:40,173 --> 00:06:44,940
The storefront controversy concerned one
part of a business increasingly focused on

100
00:06:44,980 --> 00:06:45,980
another.

101
00:06:47,251 --> 00:06:52,177
Travel retail offered WHSmith a
different operating environment.

102
00:06:52,224 --> 00:06:56,864
Airport passengers and railway commuters
often shop because they need something

103
00:06:56,904 --> 00:06:58,014
during a journey.

104
00:06:58,364 --> 00:07:04,062
Books, snacks, drinks, travel accessories,
and convenience products serve immediate

105
00:07:04,102 --> 00:07:05,102
needs.

106
00:07:05,379 --> 00:07:09,619
High-street customers have a broader set of
alternatives, including online retailers

107
00:07:09,659 --> 00:07:11,869
and competing stores.

108
00:07:12,470 --> 00:07:18,732
WHSmith had spent years expanding its
travel presence, including internationally.

109
00:07:18,772 --> 00:07:22,189
Its strategy increasingly
emphasized those locations.

110
00:07:22,894 --> 00:07:26,886
The business wasn't simply deciding
what its signs should say.

111
00:07:27,323 --> 00:07:32,134
It was deciding which retail markets deserved
its investment, management attention,

112
00:07:32,404 --> 00:07:33,954
and long-term commitment.

113
00:07:35,756 --> 00:07:41,941
On March twenty-eighth, 2025, WHSmith
announced a much more consequential change.

114
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:47,281
It had agreed to sell its UK High
Street business to Modella Capital.

115
00:07:47,760 --> 00:07:53,583
The transaction carried an announced
enterprise value of seventy-six million pounds

116
00:07:53,623 --> 00:07:55,513
on a cash-and-debt-free basis.

117
00:07:56,070 --> 00:08:01,524
This did not mean the buyer simply handed
WHSmith seventy-six million pounds in

118
00:08:01,564 --> 00:08:02,564
cash.

119
00:08:03,096 --> 00:08:05,634
The financial structure
was more complicated.

120
00:08:06,278 --> 00:08:10,577
The deal involved hundreds of
stores and thousands of employees.

121
00:08:11,158 --> 00:08:15,223
Most importantly, it marked the planned
separation of the traditional high-street

122
00:08:15,263 --> 00:08:18,193
chain from the group
that had built its name.

123
00:08:19,841 --> 00:08:22,765
There was a remarkable
detail in the transaction.

124
00:08:23,351 --> 00:08:28,933
WHSmith sold the High Street operations,
but retained ownership of the WHSmith

125
00:08:28,973 --> 00:08:30,083
brand.

126
00:08:30,591 --> 00:08:34,335
Its Travel division would
continue using the familiar name.

127
00:08:34,745 --> 00:08:38,230
The stores being sold would
eventually need another identity.

128
00:08:38,771 --> 00:08:44,094
This was not the same decision
as the 2023 WHS signage trial.

129
00:08:44,566 --> 00:08:48,047
It was a separate corporate transaction,
involving different commercial

130
00:08:48,087 --> 00:08:49,087
considerations.

131
00:08:49,815 --> 00:08:51,912
Yet the contrast was striking.

132
00:08:52,492 --> 00:08:57,379
The business associated with Britain's
high streets was changing ownership, while

133
00:08:57,419 --> 00:09:00,429
the historic name would
continue serving travelers.

134
00:09:00,962 --> 00:09:04,745
The identity and the original
retail estate were being separated.

135
00:09:06,593 --> 00:09:09,385
The sale was completed in June 2025.

136
00:09:10,284 --> 00:09:15,366
Under Modella Capital's ownership, the
former WHSmith High Street stores began

137
00:09:15,406 --> 00:09:18,696
transitioning to a new name: TGJones.

138
00:09:19,344 --> 00:09:23,347
The new owner presented the change as part
of a plan to reinvigorate traditional

139
00:09:23,387 --> 00:09:26,597
retail locations and expand their offer.

140
00:09:27,134 --> 00:09:31,293
Customers would still find familiar
products and services, but the storefront

141
00:09:31,333 --> 00:09:32,843
identity would change.

142
00:09:33,441 --> 00:09:38,795
A business whose name had been associated
with British shopping for generations was

143
00:09:38,835 --> 00:09:40,465
entering a new chapter.

144
00:09:40,965 --> 00:09:42,892
The irony was unmistakable.

145
00:09:43,324 --> 00:09:47,933
After a controversial trial involving three
letters, the larger high-street business

146
00:09:47,973 --> 00:09:51,143
was receiving an entirely new name.

147
00:09:52,849 --> 00:10:00,098
No evidence establishes that the 2023 WHS
signage controversy caused the 2025 sale.

148
00:10:00,541 --> 00:10:02,914
That would confuse two
different decisions.

149
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:08,419
WHSmith's own announcement identified a
strategic focus on higher-growth travel

150
00:10:08,459 --> 00:10:09,989
retail markets.

151
00:10:10,538 --> 00:10:14,165
Its financial reporting showed the
increasing importance of that division.

152
00:10:14,699 --> 00:10:19,546
The signage experiment, meanwhile, concerned
how the traditional stores communicated

153
00:10:19,586 --> 00:10:20,936
with customers.

154
00:10:21,421 --> 00:10:26,470
Both developments belong to the same broader
period of change, but they should not

155
00:10:26,510 --> 00:10:28,540
be treated as cause and effect.

156
00:10:29,080 --> 00:10:33,713
A compelling business story does
not require inventing a connection.

157
00:10:34,150 --> 00:10:37,090
The documented contrast
is interesting enough.

158
00:10:38,890 --> 00:10:41,783
WHSmith's story reveals three lessons.

159
00:10:42,348 --> 00:10:47,555
First, a recognizable name is a valuable
business asset, even when a company

160
00:10:47,595 --> 00:10:50,265
believes its presentation needs updating.

161
00:10:50,844 --> 00:10:55,199
Second, visual simplicity is not
the same as customer clarity.

162
00:10:55,745 --> 00:11:01,158
And third, a brand's identity can outlive
the retail business that made it famous.

163
00:11:01,777 --> 00:11:07,902
The WHS trial generated criticism because
customers and commentators recognized

164
00:11:07,942 --> 00:11:09,812
something had changed.

165
00:11:10,366 --> 00:11:15,400
The later sale showed that the company's
actual strategic transformation went far

166
00:11:15,440 --> 00:11:17,250
beyond storefront design.

167
00:11:17,855 --> 00:11:19,848
One story was about perception.

168
00:11:20,491 --> 00:11:25,256
The other was about where WHSmith believed
its future growth would come from.

169
00:11:26,926 --> 00:11:31,982
For more than two centuries, WHSmith
built recognition through its presence in

170
00:11:32,022 --> 00:11:34,002
everyday British life.

171
00:11:34,042 --> 00:11:37,972
Then a three-letter experiment
became a national talking point.

172
00:11:38,408 --> 00:11:41,450
But the bigger transformation
was not a logo.

173
00:11:41,987 --> 00:11:45,724
It was the decision to separate the
company's traditional high-street operations

174
00:11:45,764 --> 00:11:48,854
from its growing travel business.

175
00:11:49,406 --> 00:11:52,273
The lesson is not that
brands should never change.

176
00:11:52,913 --> 00:11:58,870
It is that every change needs a clear
purpose, and every outcome needs to be judged

177
00:11:58,910 --> 00:12:00,060
against evidence.

178
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:05,959
A familiar name can survive even when the
business behind it changes completely.

179
00:12:06,622 --> 00:12:08,968
And every brand leaves evidence.

